==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From the {{der|en|la|instigatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|instigare||to instigate}}, from prefix ''[[in-]]'' in + {{m|la||*stigare}}, akin to {{m|la|stinguere||push, goad}}. Akin to {{cog|de|stechen||to prick}}, {{etyl|en|-}} [[stick]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɪnstəɡeɪt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-instigate.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|instigat}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[incite]]; to bring about by urging or encouraging.
#: ''to '''instigate''' a riot''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[goad]] or [[urge]] (a person) forward, especially to wicked actions; to [[provoke]].
#: ''to '''instigate''' someone to a crime''
#* Bishop Warburton
#*: He hath only '''instigated''' his blackest agents to the very extent of their malignity.

====Synonyms====
* {{qualifier|to goad or urge forward}}: [[animate]], [[encourage]], [[impel]], [[incite]], [[provoke]], [[spur]], [[stimulate]], [[tempt]], [[urge]]

====Antonyms====
* {{qualifier|to goad or urge forward}}: [[halt]], [[prevent]], [[stop]]

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|instigation}}
* {{l|en|instigator}}

